// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
//
// The world JS/TS commands are componentized against. It exports exactly
// Pane's contract and imports its extension data, operations and installed
// applications (wit/extension.wit, wit/data.wit, wit/operations.wit and
// wit/applications.wit, copied into deps/ by the build), its native helpers
// (wit/helpers.wit), and declares the
// WASI 0.3 imports JS source may use: a JS module can import only what this
// world declares. WASI's own WIT (wit/deps: clocks, and `wasi:http` for web
// requests, with the packages it names) is copied into deps/ too. The host
// bindings are unchanged. `wasi:http`'s client is imported only by a command
// whose bundle uses it (itself or through `@pane/extension/http`): the build
// adds it to `js-command`, so a command that makes no web requests does not
// import it, and Pane does not list it as using the network.
//
// A command whose package.json sets `"pane": { ... }` options also exports
// the interfaces they name: `rootResults` (wit/root-results.wit),
// `indexedResults` (wit/applications.wit), `operations`
// (wit/operations.wit), `takesQuery` (wit/query.wit), `search`
// (wit/search.wit) and `scheduledTask` (wit/background.wit); `files` makes
// it import the granted folder's listing
// (wit/files.wit), which no other command imports. The build writes a world
// `js-command` including this one with those exports and imports
// (tools/componentize-js/pane_js.py).
package pane:js-guest@0.1.0;

world js-extension {
  include pane:extension/extension-with-data@0.1.0;
  import pane:extension/applications@0.1.0;
  import pane:extension/helpers@0.1.0;
  import wasi:clocks/monotonic-clock@0.3.0;
}
